                    round_name_r1:          `Diagnose`,
                    round_name_r2:          `Presence & Conversation`,
                    round_name_r3:          `Development Action`,
                    round_name_r4:          `Recover & Follow Through`,

                    briefing_title_r1:      `Round 1 — Read the signal`,
                    briefing_text_r1:       `Priya’s message is <strong>ambiguous on purpose</strong>: it could be a pay grievance, a manager-relationship problem, a passing mood, or a genuine <em>career-regret</em> signal — a quiet sense she may be on the wrong path. The exit-interview themes at Aldgate point one way: talented people leave <strong>not over pay, but over feeling on the wrong path</strong>. Weigh the evidence — the missed promotion, the sideways move 18 months ago, the standup silence — name the single most important unknown to test, and read your own credibility honestly: you are <em>eight weeks in</em> and the whole team is watching.`,
                    briefing_title_r2:      `Round 2 — Presence & the conversation`,
                    briefing_text_r2:       `Now decide <strong>how you show up</strong>. Presence is a finite, signalling resource — a routine update warrants a quick call, but a <em>career conversation</em> may be worth the trip to the Manchester hub. The client deadline is real, but her recruiter call is in five days: <strong>parking her behind the deadline closes the window</strong>. Design the conversation as a structured <em>stay conversation</em> — open questions that surface fit and meaning, with nothing over-promised — and remember the rest of the nine-person team is reading what your response says about how careers get attention here.`,
                    briefing_title_r3:      `Round 3 — Choose the development action`,
                    briefing_text_r3:       `Commit a response that fits the <strong>diagnosed root cause, not the symptom</strong>. For a career-regret problem, <em>role re-crafting</em> — reshaping the tasks, relationships and meaning of the role — is the primary, low-cost lever. Avoid the over-correction (<strong>buying a meaning problem with money</strong>) and the under-correction (a vague “let’s chat again”). Then run the feasibility check: as an eight-week leader you can authorise some things yourself and must <em>escalate</em> the rest to the skip-level director and People Partner — promising what you can’t deliver damages credibility more than saying nothing.`,
                    briefing_title_r4:      `Round 4 — Build the adjustment & recover credibility`,
                    briefing_text_r4:       `Present the <strong>90-day plan</strong> to the skip-level director. A retention is not a single good conversation — show the re-crafted role checkpoints, the <em>leading indicators</em> that tell you the regret signal is resolving, and the scheduled follow-through. Show how the episode <strong>rebuilds your credibility with the whole team</strong>, and connect retaining and developing a high-potential woman to Aldgate’s leadership-pipeline goal — honestly, not as a numbers tick.`,

                    r1_diag_label:          `After weighing the evidence, what is Priya’s signal most likely telling you?`,
                    r1_test_label:          `What is the single most important unknown to test before you act?`,
                    r1_cred_label:          `Eight weeks in, how do you read your own credibility position?`,
                    r1_confidence_label:    `How confident are you that you have actually diagnosed the root cause (not the symptom)? (%)`,
                    r1_confidence_readout:  `Diagnostic confidence:`,
                    r1_pct:                 `%`,
                    diag_regret:            `A career-regret / identity signal — a quiet sense she may be on the wrong path, consistent with her sideways move and the exit-interview themes`,
                    diag_relationship:      `A manager-relationship problem — she has gone quiet because of how she’s being managed`,
                    diag_pay:               `A pay grievance — she was passed over and wants more money or a faster title`,
                    diag_mood:              `A passing mood after a missed cycle — it will blow over once the deadline clears`,
                    test_meaning:           `What “this” means to her — whether the work still fits who she wants to become`,
                    test_recruiter:         `How serious the recruiter conversation is — find out how close she is to leaving`,
                    test_none:              `Nothing material is unknown — the message speaks for itself, act now`,
                    credself_forming:       `Thin and forming — the team is watching this as evidence of whether careers are safe with me`,
                    credself_proven:        `Already established — I was promoted on merit, the team trusts me`,
                    credself_irrelevant:    `Not relevant here — this is about Priya, not about me`,

                    r2_modality_label:      `Choose the channel and setting for the career conversation.`,
                    r2_timing_label:        `When do you hold it, given the competing client deadline?`,
                    r2_design_label:        `Design the conversation itself — what is it built around?`,
                    r2_team_label:          `What does the rest of the nine-person team see in how you handle this?`,
                    modality_inperson:      `Invest in presence — a deliberate in-person Manchester-hub day for a proper, unhurried conversation`,
                    modality_quickcall:     `A quick 15-minute video call squeezed between client tasks — respects her flexibility`,
                    modality_text:          `Handle it over chat / a written message — least intrusive, keeps it low-key`,
                    timing_thisweek:        `This week, inside the 5-day window — make 90 minutes before the recruiter call`,
                    timing_afterdeadline:   `After the client deadline — deal with the urgent first, talk to her next week`,
                    timing_vague:           `Leave it open — tell her “let’s grab time soon” without fixing a slot`,
                    design_stay:            `A structured stay conversation — open questions that surface fit, meaning and what would make the role hers, with nothing over-promised`,
                    design_retain:          `Persuasion to stay — lead with reasons Aldgate is great and why she should not leave`,
                    design_interrogate:     `Direct questions about the recruiter and her plans — find out if she’s leaving`,
                    team_signal:            `A fair signal — quietly model that the team’s careers get real attention here, without singling anyone out or neglecting the others`,
                    team_hidden:            `Nothing — keep it entirely private, the team need not know careers get this attention`,
                    team_allin:             `Everything — visibly drop your other work and pour days into Priya so people see you care`,

                    r3_action_label:        `Choose the primary development action that fits the diagnosed root cause.`,
                    r3_authority_label:     `Feasibility check: what do you commit to yourself versus escalate?`,
                    r3_visibility_label:    `Add a sponsorship / visibility element?`,
                    r3_spend_label:         `Discretionary spend you commit this round, against the ~£95k cost of losing her (£000s)`,
                    action_recraft:         `Role re-craft / stretch assignment — reshape the tasks, relationships and meaning of her role to fit her strengths and identity`,
                    action_coaching:        `A coaching referral — send her to the firm’s career coach to work it through externally`,
                    action_vague:           `A vague “let’s chat again in a few weeks” — keep the door open without committing to anything`,
                    action_comp:            `A compensation / promotion-timeline offer — accelerate her money and title to make her stay`,
                    authority_splitclean:   `Split it cleanly — commit only what you can authorise, and escalate the rest to the skip-level director and People Partner`,
                    authority_promiseall:   `Promise it all now — a new role, a sponsor and a promotion, so she sees you mean it`,
                    authority_commitnothing: `Commit to nothing firm — you’re only eight weeks in, so keep it all informal`,
                    visibility_escalated:   `Yes, brokered properly — surface her work to the skip-level director and propose a visibility step you can actually arrange`,
                    visibility_overpromise: `Promise her a named sponsor and a slot at the client steering committee yourself`,
                    visibility_none:        `No — visibility is a distraction from the real conversation`,
                    spend_none:             `£0 — the action is about fit and meaning, not money`,
                    spend_modest:           `£8k — a modest, justified investment (a course, a coaching package, a stretch project)`,
                    spend_heavy:            `£40k — a large retention package and an off-cycle pay bump to be safe`,

                    r4_followthrough_label: `Facing the skip-level director — what is your 90-day follow-through?`,
                    r4_credibility_label:   `How do you rebuild credibility with the whole team after this episode?`,
                    r4_pipeline_label:      `How do you connect this to Aldgate’s leadership-pipeline goal?`,
                    r4_conviction_label:    `Conviction: how firmly do you hold that an identity-fit response retains people better than a transactional one? (%)`,
                    follow_plan90:          `A real 90-day adjustment plan — re-crafted role checkpoints, agreed leading indicators, and scheduled follow-ups, not a one-off`,
                    follow_monitor:         `Watch and wait — keep an eye on her standup participation and revisit if it dips again`,
                    follow_oneoff:          `A single good conversation — we talked, she feels heard, that should hold her`,
                    credrebuild_model:      `Model the standard quietly — make career conversations a normal team rhythm so people see careers get attention here, fairly`,
                    credrebuild_silent:     `Say nothing broadly — handling Priya well is enough, the rest will infer it`,
                    credrebuild_broadcast:  `Announce what you did for Priya so everyone sees you fought for her`,
                    pipeline_connect:       `Connect it honestly — retaining and developing a high-potential woman is the pipeline goal made real at the team level`,
                    pipeline_tokenise:      `Lead with it — frame keeping Priya mainly as a diversity-numbers win for the firm`,
                    pipeline_ignore:        `Don’t — one analyst’s case has nothing to do with the firm’s pipeline`,

                    warn_confidence_range:  `Diagnostic confidence must be a percentage between 0 and 100.`,
                    warn_choose_all:        `Choose a modality, timing, conversation design and team-visibility option.`,
                    warn_spend_value:       `Discretionary spend cannot be negative.`,
                    warn_conviction_range:  `Conviction must be a percentage between 0 and 100.`,

                    coach_default:          `Decision logged.`,
                    coach_r1_regret:        `<strong>That’s the read the evidence supports.</strong> The missed cycle, the sideways move 18 months ago, the standup silence and Aldgate’s exit-interview themes all point at <em>career regret</em> — a felt mismatch between her work and who she wants to become — not a pay or relationship problem. Diagnose that, and the right response opens up.`,
                    coach_r1_relationship:  `<strong>A relationship read isn’t unreasonable — but it may be the symptom, not the cause.</strong> She went quiet <em>after</em> a missed cycle and a sideways move, and the firm’s leavers cite being on the wrong path, not their manager. Test the meaning question before you settle on this.`,
                    coach_r1_pay:           `<strong>Pay is the easiest lever and, here, the wrong diagnosis.</strong> Aldgate’s exit interviews are blunt: people leave over feeling on the wrong path, not over money. Read it as pay and you’ll reach for a comp fix that buys you nothing and may speed her exit.`,
                    coach_r1_mood:          `<strong>“It’ll blow over” is how the window closes.</strong> A flat “whether this is really for me,” a quiet standup pattern and a recruiter in the wings is not a mood — it’s a signal. Wait for it to pass and you’ll be reading her resignation instead.`,
                    coach_r1_notest:        `<strong>“The message speaks for itself” skips the most important step.</strong> The signal is ambiguous by design — acting before you’ve tested what “this” means to her is exactly how managers fix the symptom and lose the person. Name the unknown first.`,
                    coach_r1_proven:        `<strong>Eight weeks in, “the team already trusts me” is optimistic.</strong> Credibility at this stage is still forming — built or lost on early high-stakes acts like this one. Reading it as already-won is how you under-invest in the very moment that decides it.`,
                    coach_r1_irrelevant:    `<strong>“This is about Priya, not me” misses half of what’s happening.</strong> The whole team is reading how you handle her career signal as evidence of whether <em>their</em> careers are safe with you. Your credibility is very much in play.`,
                    coach_r1_overconfident: `<strong>High confidence on a read you haven’t tested is overconfidence, not insight.</strong> The signal is ambiguous; certainty this early usually means you’ve fixed on the first plausible story. Lower the confidence or earn it by testing the meaning question.`,
                    coach_r1_sharp:         `<strong>Sharp diagnosis.</strong> You read it as a career-regret signal and named the right unknown to test — what “this” means to her, whether the work still fits who she wants to become. That’s the difference between treating the cause and chasing the symptom.`,
                    coach_r2_parked:        `<strong>The client deadline is real — so you parked Priya until next week.</strong> But her recruiter call is in five days. You optimised the urgent over the important and let the window close; by the time you’re free, she’s decided. Twenty minutes today would have changed the odds.`,
                    coach_r2_vague:         `<strong>“Let’s grab time soon” is parking it with a smile.</strong> Without a fixed slot inside the five-day window, the conversation slips behind the deadline and the recruiter gets there first. Vague timing on a live flight risk is the same as no timing.`,
                    coach_r2_inperson:      `<strong>You decided this one was worth the trip.</strong> A career conversation is not a status update — presence lets you actually read her, and signals the conversation matters. That’s the deliberate allocation of a finite resource, not presence for its own sake.`,
                    coach_r2_quickcall:     `<strong>A 15-minute call between client tasks is defensible on flexibility — and thin for a career conversation.</strong> You can’t really read her over a squeezed video call, and she’ll feel slotted in. When is presence worth the trip? This was probably one of those times.`,
                    coach_r2_text:          `<strong>Handling a career-regret signal over chat is the lightest possible touch — and the wrong one.</strong> Written messages strip the read you most need and signal the conversation didn’t warrant your presence. This is not the moment for low-key.`,
                    coach_r2_stay:          `<strong>That’s a stay conversation, not a sales pitch.</strong> Open questions that surface fit and meaning — and the discipline of <em>not</em> over-promising — are what let her tell you the truth. The goal isn’t “make her stay at all costs”; it’s to understand and adjust.`,
                    coach_r2_retain:        `<strong>Leading with “why Aldgate is great” turns a stay conversation into a sales pitch.</strong> She didn’t ask to be persuaded; she signalled doubt about her path. Persuasion closes down the very disclosure you need and tells her you weren’t really listening.`,
                    coach_r2_interrogate:   `<strong>Going straight at the recruiter and her plans reads as an interrogation.</strong> It pushes her to defend a decision she may not have made, and surfaces nothing about fit or meaning. You’ll learn whether she’s leaving, not why — and lose trust doing it.`,
                    coach_r2_allin:         `<strong>Pouring days into Priya is visible — and your other high-potential just watched you drop everything for one person.</strong> Individual interventions have team-level signals. Over-investing in one career reads to the rest as: attention here is scarce and you have to be a flight risk to get it.`,
                    coach_r3_recraft:       `<strong>Re-crafting the role is the lever that fits a regret problem.</strong> Reshaping the tasks, relationships and meaning of the work to her strengths and identity addresses the cause without losing her — and it’s low-cost. This is the identity-based response the diagnosis pointed to.`,
                    coach_r3_vague:         `<strong>“Let’s chat again in a few weeks” is the under-correction.</strong> It commits to nothing, resolves nothing, and tells her the signal didn’t really land. A vague follow-up on a five-day window is functionally a no — and she has a recruiter who is being specific.`,
                    coach_r3_comp:          `<strong>Reaching for money is the easy lever.</strong> Even if you hadn’t pinned the diagnosis, a comp-and-title fix signals that pay buys loyalty and addresses none of the fit question. If the problem turns out to be meaning, you’ve spent budget and bought nothing.`,
                    coach_r3_comp_regret:   `<strong>You diagnosed career regret — then offered money and a faster title.</strong> A faster route down a path she’s questioning makes the regret worse; she’ll take it and leave in six months, more expensively. This is the over-correction the exit interviews warn about.`,
                    coach_r3_split:         `<strong>That’s credible commitment within real authority.</strong> Committing only what you can actually authorise and escalating the rest to the skip-level director and People Partner is exactly what an eight-week leader can stand behind. Promises you can keep build more trust than grand ones you can’t.`,
                    coach_r3_promiseall:    `<strong>You — eight weeks in — promised a new role, a sponsor and a promotion.</strong> Lovely conversation; you can authorise none of it alone. If it doesn’t materialise, your credibility is worse than if you’d said nothing. What could you actually commit to, and what needed your skip-level director?`,
                    coach_r3_commitnothing: `<strong>Committing to nothing is the mirror of over-promising.</strong> Eight weeks in is a reason to escalate, not to go silent. An all-informal response gives her nothing concrete to stay for and reads as a polite brush-off.`,
                    coach_r3_visover:       `<strong>Promising a named sponsor and a steering-committee slot yourself is over-reach.</strong> Visibility is the right instinct, but you can’t hand out a sponsor on your own authority. Broker it properly through the skip-level director, or it becomes another promise you can’t keep.`,
                    coach_r3_heavyspend:    `<strong>A £40k package on a meaning problem is buying the wrong thing.</strong> Large spend signals pay-buys-loyalty and burns budget you may not have, while leaving the fit question untouched. The re-craft lever costs almost nothing and addresses the actual cause.`,
                    coach_r4_plan90:        `<strong>That’s a retention, not a moment.</strong> Re-crafted role checkpoints, agreed leading indicators and scheduled follow-ups over 90 days are what turn one good conversation into a resolved regret signal. Follow-through is the credibility, not the conversation alone.`,
                    coach_r4_monitor:       `<strong>“Watch and wait” is better than nothing and short of a plan.</strong> Monitoring standup participation catches a relapse late; it doesn’t actively adjust her path. Leading indicators are for steering, not just for noticing she’s slipping again.`,
                    coach_r4_oneoff:        `<strong>One good conversation doesn’t hold a regret signal.</strong> Without a 90-day adjustment and follow-through, the felt mismatch returns and the recruiter call comes back around. A retention that isn’t followed through is a deferred exit.`,
                    coach_r4_model:         `<strong>That rebuilds credibility the durable way.</strong> Making career conversations a normal, fair team rhythm tells the whole nine-person team their careers get attention here — without singling anyone out. Credibility is rebuilt by the standard you set, not the announcement you make.`,
                    coach_r4_broadcast:     `<strong>Announcing what you did for Priya backfires.</strong> It reads as taking credit and tells the others attention is scarce and personal rather than a standard. Credibility comes from the rhythm you set quietly, not the broadcast.`,
                    coach_r4_connect:       `<strong>That connects the individual to the pipeline honestly.</strong> Retaining and developing one high-potential woman <em>is</em> Aldgate’s leadership-pipeline goal made real at the team level — the system problem solved one credible manager-moment at a time.`,
                    coach_r4_tokenise:      `<strong>Framing Priya mainly as a diversity-numbers win reduces her to a metric.</strong> The pipeline goal is real, but leading with the tick rather than her development is how good intentions read as tokenism — and she will feel it. Develop the person; the pipeline follows.`,

                    sum_default:            `Decision made.`,
                    sum_r1_good:            `Read it as career regret; named the meaning question to test.`,
                    sum_r1_weak:            `Mis-read the signal or skipped testing the key unknown.`,
