                    sum_r4_good:            `90-day plan; modelled the standard; connected to the pipeline.`,
                    sum_r4_weak:            `Follow-through, credibility move or pipeline framing under-tuned.`,

                    insight_navigated:      `Crossroads navigated: you read the career-regret signal rather than the symptom, showed up in person inside the five-day window, ran a real stay conversation, re-crafted Priya’s role to fit her identity, committed only what you could authorise and escalated the rest, then followed through over 90 days. Identity-fit retention, done credibly — the model the exit interviews say Aldgate keeps missing.`,
                    insight_parked:         `Window closed: you optimised the urgent client deadline over the important career conversation, and Priya’s recruiter call came first. The expensive lesson — ~£95k to replace her and a £1.2M renewal exposed — is that twenty deliberate minutes inside the window beat a perfect plan delivered too late.`,
                    insight_transactional:  `Bought the wrong problem: you treated a meaning-and-fit signal as a pay problem and reached for money or a title. A faster route down a path she’s questioning deepens the regret — she takes the package and leaves anyway, more expensively. Transactional fixes don’t resolve occupational regret.`,
                    insight_blind:          `Acted before diagnosing: you committed a development action without ever establishing what Priya’s signal meant. Fixing a symptom you never identified is how managers spend effort and budget and still lose the person — the diagnosis was the whole point.`,
                    insight_overpromised:   `Credibility damaged: eight weeks in, you promised roles, sponsors or promotions you couldn’t authorise. When commitments don’t materialise, you are worse off than if you’d said nothing — credible commitment within real authority is what an emerging leader is actually building.`,
                    insight_virtual:        `Default-virtual avoidance: you ran a career conversation over a quick call or message to respect flexibility, and got a read too thin to act on. Presence is a finite, signalling resource — this was one of the moments it was worth spending.`,
                    insight_mixed:          `A mixed outcome: parts of the response landed, but the diagnosis, presence, action and follow-through didn’t fully reinforce each other — enough gaps that the regret signal isn’t convincingly resolved and the leader-credibility gain is partial.`,
                    insight_nodiagnosis:    `You never landed the career-regret diagnosis: without reading the signal as a fit-and-meaning question, every later choice was aimed at the wrong target, and the most likely root cause went unaddressed.`,
                    insight_cred_low:       `Leader credibility stayed low: eight weeks in, this high-stakes act was the team’s evidence of whether their careers are safe with you, and the way it was handled left that question open.`,
                    insight_stay_low:       `Priya’s stay probability never recovered: the combination of an unresolved regret signal, thin credibility or a closed window left her more likely to take the recruiter’s call than to re-engage.`,
                    insight_lowcost:        `Notably, you achieved this at very low cost — the re-craft lever resolves a regret signal for almost nothing, which is exactly why money is the wrong first instinct here.`,

                    hint_parked:            `You let the five-day window close by putting the deadline first — regret resolution is now capped because the decision is effectively hers. Next time, find twenty minutes inside the window.`,
                    hint_transactional:     `You’re treating a meaning problem as a pay problem — a faster route down the wrong path deepens the regret. Pivot to a fit-based response: re-craft the role, don’t buy loyalty.`,
                    hint_overpromised:      `You’ve promised beyond your authority — credibility is capped until you separate what you can commit from what must be escalated to the skip-level director.`,
                    hint_diagnose:          `You still haven’t diagnosed the signal as career regret — every action you take is aimed at a symptom. Read the cause before you commit the response.`,
                    hint_virtual:           `You’re running a career conversation on a thin channel — your read of her is too shallow to act on. Treat presence as a resource worth spending on this one.`,
                    hint_strong:            `Strong position — the regret signal is resolving, Priya is likely to stay and your credibility is climbing. Land it with a real 90-day plan and a fair team-wide standard.`,

